    <section>
      <title>Three Ways to Point to One Paragraph</title>
      <slide>
        <title>Way 1: Plain Link</title>
        <p>Read the second paragraph of Section 5.1.1, which looks back at Section 4.1 and the Total Change Theorem:</p>
        <p><url href="https://activecalculus.org/single2e/sec-5-1-antid-graphs.html#sec-5-1-antid-graphs-3-2">Section 5.1.1, second paragraph</url></p>
      </slide>
      <slide>
        <title>Way 2: Screenshot That Is a Link</title>
        <p><url href="https://activecalculus.org/single2e/sec-5-1-antid-graphs.html#sec-5-1-antid-graphs-3-2"><image source="paragraph-screenshot.png" width="85%"><description>Screenshot of the second paragraph of Section 5.1.1 of Active Calculus. It recalls that Section 4.1 started from a moving object's velocity to learn about its position, and that the Total Change Theorem says the total change in f over an interval from a to b equals the net signed area between the graph of f prime and the x-axis. Select to open the paragraph in the book. (Sample text: paste the paragraph's exact words here if you want it read verbatim.)</description></image></url></p>
      </slide>
      <slide>
        <title>Way 3: Embedded Preview</title>
